PythonTypeScript <pre><code>def validate_tabular(envelope: TabularEnvelope) -&gt; list[Finding]: ...
</code></pre> <pre><code>function validateTabular(envelope: TabularEnvelope): Finding[];
</code></pre> <p>An opt-in check exported from the package root: <code>TabularEnvelope</code> never calls it on parse, so a large table pays no cost on read and one bad cell never rejects a whole result. It checks every column's cells against its declared kind and returns at most one <code>Finding</code> per bad column, in column order: <code>rule</code> is <code>cell_kind</code>, <code>path</code> is a JSON pointer to the first bad cell (<code>/rows/3/2</code>), <code>tool</code> is <code>None</code> (the caller may <code>replace</code> it), and <code>message</code> names the column, the kind, the first bad value and the failure count (<code>column 'size' (big_integer): 1204 of 50000 cells do not fit; first at row 3: 'n/a'</code>). A <code>None</code> cell always passes, under any kind. An envelope with no <code>column_kinds</code> has nothing to check and returns <code>[]</code>.</p> <p>A producer asserts its own tabular results fit their declared kinds with one test line:</p> PythonTypeScript <pre><code>assert validate_tabular(TabularEnvelope.model_validate(result)) == []
</code></pre> <pre><code>expect(validateTabular(TabularEnvelope.parse(result))).toEqual([]);
</code></pre> <p>Each kind accepts:</p> Kind A cell passes when it is <code>text</code> a <code>str</code> <code>number</code> an <code>int</code> or a finite <code>float</code>, not a <code>bool</code> <code>integer</code> an <code>int</code>, not a <code>bool</code>, with magnitude at most 2^53 - 1 (a larger value belongs in <code>big_integer</code>) <code>boolean</code> a <code>bool</code> <code>timestamp</code> a <code>str</code> in RFC 3339 <code>date-time</code> form: date, <code>T</code>, time, and a <code>Z</code> or numeric offset <code>ip</code> a <code>str</code> that <code>ipaddress.ip_address</code> accepts <code>fqdn</code> a <code>str</code> of dot-separated labels, each 1-63 of letters, digits and <code>-</code> with no leading or trailing <code>-</code>, at most 253 in total, with an optional trailing dot <code>mac</code> a <code>str</code> of six or eight hex pairs joined consistently by <code>:</code> or <code>-</code> <code>ip_block</code> a <code>str</code> that is an address, an <code>ip_network(strict=False)</code> prefix, or <code>first-last</code> with both addresses in one family and <code>first &lt;= last</code> <code>big_integer</code> an <code>int</code> (not a <code>bool</code>), or a <code>str</code> matching <code>^-?(0\|[1-9][0-9]*)$</code> <p>The same paging fields as the records envelope apply - optional <code>total_count</code>, <code>next_cursor</code> and <code>stop_reason</code>, and the computed <code>resumable</code> - with <code>row_count</code> as the number returned; the optional ones are likewise omitted when absent. <code>TabularEnvelope</code> is published once so a tool that consumes another tool's table declares its argument as the model and gets <code>{"$ref": "#/$defs/TabularEnvelope"}</code> in its input schema instead of redefining <code>columns</code> and <code>rows</code>:</p> PythonTypeScript <pre><code>class ChartArgs(BaseModel):
    data: TabularEnvelope = Field(description="A table another tool returned.")
    title: str = Field(description="Chart title.")
</code></pre> <pre><code>const ChartArgs = z.object({
  data: TabularEnvelope.describe("A table another tool returned."),
  title: z.string().describe("Chart title."),
});
</code></pre>

PythonTypeScript <pre><code>def read_envelope(model: type[E], data: Mapping[str, object] | str | bytes) -&gt; E: ...
</code></pre> <pre><code>function readEnvelope&lt;O&gt;(model: z.ZodType&lt;O&gt;, data: unknown): O;
</code></pre> <p>A function exported from the package root, for any code parsing an envelope it did not construct - <code>E</code> is <code>RecordsEnvelope</code> (any parametrization), <code>TabularEnvelope</code> or <code>ArtifactEnvelope</code>; in TypeScript, <code>model</code> is <code>TabularEnvelope</code>, <code>ArtifactEnvelope</code> or a schema <code>RecordsEnvelope(item)</code> returned. <code>data</code> is a <code>Mapping</code>, or a <code>str</code> / <code>bytes</code> parsed as JSON; in TypeScript, an object, a string, or a <code>Uint8Array</code> decoded as UTF-8. It validates the same model as <code>model_validate</code> (<code>.parse</code>), with two relaxations:</p> <ul> <li>Unknown keys are dropped, at the envelope level and in a nested   <code>octools</code> model (<code>ArtifactRef</code>). A key inside <code>records</code> is untouched:   the record model belongs to the caller, and its own config decides.</li> <li>Unknown vocabulary values degrade to a fallback. <code>read_envelope</code>   applies this to the envelope vocabularies, <code>column_kinds</code>,   <code>column_scales</code> and <code>sensitivity</code>:</li> </ul> Vocabulary Unknown value reads as <code>column_kinds</code> <code>"text"</code> <code>column_scales</code> <code>None</code> <code>sensitivity</code> <code>"inherits_input"</code> <p>A scale on a column whose kind is non-numeric, whose kind degraded to <code>"text"</code>, or where <code>column_kinds</code> is absent altogether, reads as <code>None</code> rather than failing the numeric-column check - the scale is a hint about a numeric kind, and dropping it is always safe once the kind it describes is unreadable or absent. Every other check still runs: types, required fields, row widths, the paging rules (a <code>resumable</code> that contradicts <code>next_cursor</code> included), and the length agreement on <code>column_scales</code>. A malformed <code>column_descriptions</code> is dropped rather than rejected (see <code>column_descriptions</code>).</p> <p>Every closed vocabulary declares a fallback, and every fallback is the most conservative reading of its vocabulary - a value in a future release that is more restrictive than a current fallback needs a new major, so a tolerant reader never treats data as less sensitive, or a tool as less remote, than it is. Two more fallbacks apply to fields <code>read_envelope</code> does not touch, because they belong to the <code>OCTool</code> descriptor rather than an envelope, and <code>octools</code> has no descriptor reader of its own: any code reading <code>result_kind</code> or <code>access</code> back out of an MCP tool's <code>_meta</code> applies the same rule.</p> Field (source) Unknown value reads as <code>result_kind</code> (<code>_meta</code>) <code>"records"</code> <code>access</code> (<code>_meta</code>) <code>"remote"</code> <p><code>VOCABULARY_FALLBACKS</code> maps the envelope field names <code>column_kinds</code>, <code>column_scales</code> and <code>sensitivity</code> to their fallbacks. <code>COLUMN_KIND_FALLBACK</code> (<code>"text"</code>), <code>COLUMN_SCALE_FALLBACK</code> (<code>None</code>), <code>SENSITIVITY_FALLBACK</code> (<code>"inherits_input"</code>), <code>RESULT_KIND_FALLBACK</code> (<code>"records"</code>) and <code>ACCESS_FALLBACK</code> (<code>"remote"</code>) are the same five values by name, for callers that check one field at a time. The <code>x-sensitivity</code> convention on record fields has the same shape: an unrecognized value reads as <code>"secret"</code>, its most sensitive value (see Sensitivity on record fields).</p> <p>Constructing an envelope, or calling <code>model_validate</code> (<code>.parse</code>) on one, stays strict: an unknown key or vocabulary value raises there, so a producer's typo still fails in the producer's own tests. <code>read_envelope</code> raises <code>TypeError</code> when <code>model</code> is not one of the three envelope types.</p> PythonTypeScript <pre><code>from octools import TabularEnvelope, read_envelope

table = read_envelope(TabularEnvelope, text)
</code></pre> <pre><code>import { TabularEnvelope, readEnvelope } from "@opscogs/octools";

const table = readEnvelope(TabularEnvelope, text);
</code></pre>

PythonTypeScript <pre><code>def summarize_provider(
    provider: ToolProvider, *, access: Access | None = None
) -&gt; tuple[ToolSummary, ...]: ...
</code></pre> <pre><code>function summarizeProvider(
  provider: ListedToolProvider,
  options?: SummarizeOptions, // { access? }
): ToolSummary[];
</code></pre> <p>Summarizes every tool <code>provider.all_tools()</code> returns, in that order, filtered to <code>access</code> ("local" or "remote") when given.</p>

PythonTypeScript <pre><code>def render_markdown_table(
    rows: Sequence[Mapping[str, object]], columns: Sequence[str]
) -&gt; str: ...
def render_listing(
    provider: ToolProvider,
    *,
    style: ListingStyle = "table",
    access: Access | None = None,
    pretty: bool = True,
) -&gt; str: ...
</code></pre> <pre><code>function renderMarkdownTable(
  rows: readonly Readonly&lt;Record&lt;string, unknown&gt;&gt;[],
  columns: readonly string[],
): string;
function renderListing(
  provider: ListedToolProvider,
  options?: RenderListingOptions, // { style?, access?, pretty? }
): string;
</code></pre> <p><code>ListedToolProvider</code> is any object whose <code>allTools()</code> lists tools; every <code>ToolProvider</code> is one.</p> <p><code>render_markdown_table</code> pads a GitHub-flavored table over any rows and column names: a <code>bool</code> cell renders as <code>yes</code> / <code>no</code>, <code>None</code> or a missing key as an empty cell, anything else via <code>str()</code>; a literal <code>|</code> in a cell is escaped as <code>\|</code> and a newline becomes a space. With no rows it renders the header and divider only. The result always ends with a newline.</p> <p><code>render_listing</code> is the one call behind the <code>octools list</code> command: given a provider and a style (one of the formats above), it summarizes and renders in one step. An unknown style raises <code>ValueError</code> (a <code>RangeError</code> in TypeScript) naming the valid ones, <code>LISTING_STYLES</code> (<code>"table"</code>, <code>"md"</code>, <code>"txt"</code>, <code>"json"</code>, also exported as the <code>ListingStyle</code> literal); <code>markdown</code> is a CLI alias only. <code>pretty</code> applies to <code>json</code> alone: indented when true (the default), compact on one line when false. The result ends with a newline, except that an empty listing renders as an empty string for <code>table</code> and <code>txt</code>; <code>md</code> then renders the header and divider only, and <code>json</code> renders <code>[]\n</code>.</p>
